WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.069
Picture an insurance claims agent that takes twenty steps per claim.

2
00:00:04.399 --> 00:00:09.106
Every step re-sends the same long system prompt, plus everything seen so far.

3
00:00:09.906 --> 00:00:13.675
One Monday, an engineer adds the current time to the prompt's first line.

4
00:00:14.005 --> 00:00:17.069
By Friday, every step is slower, and the bill has jumped.

5
00:00:17.869 --> 00:00:21.273
Worse, by step fifteen the agent forgets the policy number,

6
00:00:21.323 --> 00:00:22.854
buried under raw documents.

7
00:00:23.184 --> 00:00:26.014
The model is fine; the problem is what it was handed.

8
00:00:26.814 --> 00:00:31.095
A solid system prompt has six parts, from role and scope to termination.

9
00:00:31.425 --> 00:00:35.407
It says what the agent won't do, when to escalate, and what done looks like.

10
00:00:36.207 --> 00:00:40.018
Put everything static at the top, and everything dynamic at the bottom.

11
00:00:40.348 --> 00:00:43.276
A byte-stable prefix lets prefix caching cut

12
00:00:43.326 --> 00:00:46.186
time to first token and cost on every step.

13
00:00:46.986 --> 00:00:50.435
That timestamp on line one changed the prefix on every call.

14
00:00:50.765 --> 00:00:52.421
It silently switched the cache off.

15
00:00:53.221 --> 00:00:55.433
The bigger lever is context engineering.

16
00:00:55.763 --> 00:00:59.041
It asks what belongs in the window at this step, and what doesn't.

17
00:00:59.841 --> 00:01:04.868
Big tool results are stored in full, with just a digest and a reference in context.

18
00:01:05.198 --> 00:01:07.943
And the agent sees only the tools for its current phase.

19
00:01:08.743 --> 00:01:11.146
Old turns get replaced by a running summary.

20
00:01:11.476 --> 00:01:13.410
Key facts, like the policy number,

21
00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:16.503
live in structured state, so they survive compaction.

22
00:01:17.303 --> 00:01:21.051
Fetch a policy when it's needed, instead of hoping the model recalls it.

23
00:01:21.381 --> 00:01:24.872
Put key evidence at the start and end, because the middle gets lost.

24
00:01:25.672 --> 00:01:28.801
To teach an agent how to choose, examples beat rules.

25
00:01:29.131 --> 00:01:32.731
Show a few full runs, including a tool failure it recovers from,

26
00:01:32.781 --> 00:01:34.499
and a moment to ask a question.

27
00:01:35.299 --> 00:01:37.404
A prompt is not a security boundary.

28
00:01:37.734 --> 00:01:41.524
Enforcement belongs in guardrails and tool-level authorisation.

29
00:01:42.324 --> 00:01:47.159
Treat prompts like code: version them, and gate changes on a golden regression set.

30
00:01:47.489 --> 00:01:51.045
A one-word edit can shift tool accuracy by several points.

31
00:01:51.845 --> 00:01:54.792
Exam tip: keep the static prefix byte-identical,

32
00:01:54.842 --> 00:01:58.600
because a timestamp at the top quietly breaks prefix caching.

33
00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:02.208
Read on for the full anatomy and every context technique.
